A1. If Friday followed Robinson Crusoe everywhere, what followed Friday? ~ SATURDAY
A8. A bunch of dates. ~ CALENDAR
A15. This is—me, myself, and nobody else but. ~ EGO
A16. What you should solve this puzzle with. ~ EASE
A17. [no clue printed] ~ ADIT
A18. What do most people do for their new Spring ear. ~ OWE
A19. Some people won't take this for an answer. ~ NO
A20. Town in Alaska. ~ SITKA
A21. What kind of sweeties did Cleopatra specialize in? ~ ROMAN
A23. Printer's measure. ~ EN
A24. The new leaf that scholars turn over. ~ PAGE
A25. This fellow is all wet. ~ SOT
A27. Heroic narrative. ~ EPIC
A29. This follows the bride. ~ TRAIN
A31. This would put a heavyweight champion to sleep. ~ ETHER
A33. Elevated in mind. ~ ELATE
A35. There is often a mess when these hit the canvas. ~ OILS
A36. A conjunction. ~ OR
A37. A bone. ~ OS
A39. A Mohammedan Prince. ~ EMIR
A40. A deer little thing. ~ ROE
A41. This is a cutting thing to do. ~ SNIP
A43. An official call on the bootlegger. ~ RAID
A45. Before. ~ ERE
A46. Something comic about the papers. ~ STRIP
A48. Not at all. ~ NEVER
A50. What the swain did in the bashful age. ~ OGLED
A52. An exclamation. ~ LO
A53. A river hold-up. ~ DAM
A54. Me and my shadow—a pronoun. ~ WE
A55. A poor fish. ~ SMELT
A58. What Cicero talked in the Senate. ~ LATIN
A60. A musical study. ~ ETUDE
A64. A heavyweight. ~ TON
A65. You can't get anywhere with this if you're out of wind. ~ SAIL
A67. This is apt to cause an exposure. ~ TEAR
A69. A present day Napoleon. ~ NUT
A70. On the square this has length, and breadth. ~ AREA
A72. Her troubles are little ones. ~ MA
A73. Spanish definite article. ~ EL
A74. To engage in a faction. ~ SIDE
A75. These often get lit-up. ~ LAMPS
A77. Speedy. ~ RAPID
A81. To prevent by prohibition—if it works. ~ DETER
A82. These are forever green. ~ YEWS
A84. A very low person. ~ CAD
A85. These are raised in the best families. ~ PETS
A86. Part of the verb "to be." ~ IS
A88. A pause in the Psalms. ~ SELAH
A90. Paradises. ~ EDENS
A92. To jumble. ~ PI
A93. The unit of resistance. ~ OHM
A95. Many people with a winning personality do this. ~ LOSE
A96. What certain high-hat people put on. ~ AIRS
A97. This is often walked over. ~ MAT
A98. Quite unnecessary. ~ NEEDLESS
A99. How the speedy younger generation gets along. ~ SPEEDILY

D1. These people have something on the House. ~ SENATORS
D2. This means sometime in the past. ~ AGO
D3. A preposition. ~ TO
D4. Rule. ~ REIGN
D5. You like this with a peach. ~ DATE
D6. Any bum will do this for a handout. ~ ASK
D7. This will raise the dough. ~ YEAST
D8. Gold-diggers can read this readily. ~ CARTE
D9. Bustle. ~ ADO
D10. This is quite an ade. ~ LIME
